oratory abnormalities"], "alarm_features": ["Hypercalcemia (Ca2+: 12.5 mg/dL)", "Unintentional weight loss", "Elevated alkaline phosphatase with constitutional symptoms"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Endocrine/Parathyroid Disorder with Hypercalcemia", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.042, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Elevated alkaline phosphatase is less specific for parathyroid disorders (B1) but does not definitively rule them out. This weakly reduces their likelihood."]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Malignancy with Hypercalcemia and Bone Involvement", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.5412270289797279, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Elevated alkaline phosphatase suggests bone involvement, which is seen in malignancy (B2). However, the absence of specific evidence for PTHrP or bone imaging …"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": ["humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "multiple myeloma", "squamous cell carcinoma", "primary hyperparathyroidism"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Reactive Hypercalcemia (e.g., Antacid Overuse or Exogenous Causes)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.1834862385321101, "posterior": 0.2775523225537066, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Antacid overuse (B3) can cause hypercalcemia, but elevated alkaline phosphatase is less typical, making this explanation less certain."], "evidence_against": ["Elevated alkaline phosphatase is not characteristic of antacid-induced hypercalcemia (B3), which weakly contradicts this diagnosis."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": ["primary hyperparathyroidism", "humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "milk-alkali syndrome", "sarcoidosis"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Other Systemic Disorder with Hypercalcemia and Alkaline Phosphatase Elevation", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.069, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Viral Illness with Subacute Constitutional Symptoms", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.069, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Squamous Cell Carcinoma with Humoral Hypercalcemia (PTHrP-mediated)", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.2952147430798515, "posterior": 0.2952147430798515, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["primary hyperparathyroidism", "humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "milk-alkali syndrome", "sarcoidosis"]}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Breast Cancer with Bone Metastases", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.19680982871990105, "posterior": 0.19680982871990105, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Multiple Myeloma with Lytic Bone Lesions and Hypercalcemia", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.04920245717997526, "posterior": 0.04920245717997526, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.65, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": ["primary hyperparathyroidism", "humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy", "milk-alkali syndrome", "sarcoidosis"]}}, "frontier": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3", "B3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the hypercalcemia (Ca2+: 12.5 mg/dL) with normal physical exam and subacute constitutional symptoms align with parathyroid hormone elevation in primary hyperparathyroidism?", "summary": "Hypercalcemia with normal physical exam is consistent with primary hyperparathyroidism, a parathyroid disorder (B1). The LR+ of 2.0 for hypercalcemia in this context suggests weak support."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of hypercalcemia (12.5 mg/dL), elevated alkaline phosphatase (150 U/L), and constitutional symptoms support malignancy-related bone destruction as the mechanism?", "summary": "Elevated alkaline phosphatase suggests bone involvement, which is seen in malignancy (B2). However, the absence of specific evidence for PTHrP or bone imaging findings weakens this support."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's history of poorly controlled GERD and antacid use explain the hypercalcemia without requiring elevated parathyroid hormone or PTHrP?", "summary": "Antacid overuse (B3) can cause hypercalcemia, but elevated alkaline phosphatase is less typical, making this explanation less certain."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the elevation of alkaline phosphatase (150 U/L) with subacute onset contradict parathyroid disorders as a primary cause of hypercalcemia?", "summary": "Elevated alkaline phosphatase is less specific for parathyroid disorders (B1) but does not definitively rule them out. This weakly reduces their likelihood."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of renal failure, arrhythmia, or PTHrP measurement in the vignette represent critical evidence against malignancy as the primary cause?", "summary": "Missing PTHrP data and absence of renal failure/arrhythmia are neutral for malignancy (B2), as these findings are not decisive in this context."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of elevated alkaline phosphatase (150 U/L) contradict a diagnosis of antacid-induced hypercalcemia?", "summary": "Elevated alkaline phosphatase is not characteristic of antacid-induced hypercalcemia (B3), which weakly contradicts this diagnosis."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.541], ["B2.1", 0.295], ["B3", 0.278]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No acute instability markers or time-critical syndromes identified", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch (B2.1) posterior (0.295) < 0.75, and dangerous alternatives (B2.1, B2.2, B2.3) remain unresolved with posteriors ≥0.10. Estimated_remaining_value (0.32) > 0.1, and max_turn_budget (5) has not been exhausted (1 used)."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.32, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.2952147430798515, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely explanation of this patient's current presentation?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Antacid overuse"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Increased 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Increased parathyroid hormone"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Malignancy"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Viral illness"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Elevated tissue non-specific alkaline phosphatase', 'Elevated alkaline phosphatase'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "program_faults": [], "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B2", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.541}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2.1", "label": "Squamous Cell Carcinoma with Humoral Hypercalcemia (PTHrP-mediated)", "posterior": 0.295, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B3", "B2.2", "B4"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=43%, source=both]\n\nReactive Hypercalcemia (e.g., Antacid Overuse or Exogenous Causes) vs Squamous Cell Carcinoma with Humoral Hypercalcemia (PTHrP-mediated):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, anorexia, arrhythmia, bone pain, bradycardia\n  Favours second: autosomal recessive inheritance, horn-like growth, itching, nail changes, numbness\n\nReactive Hypercalcemia (e.g., Antacid Overuse or Exogenous Causes) vs Breast Cancer with Bone Metastases:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, arrhythmia, bradycardia, calcium deposits in kidneys, coma\n  Favours second: abdominal distention, abdominal situs inversus, abnormal atrial arrangement, abnormal axonemal organization of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal central microtubular pair morphology of respiratory motile cilia\n\nSquamous Cell Carcinoma with Humoral Hypercalcemia (PTHrP-mediated) vs Breast Cancer with Bone Metastases:\n  Favours first:  horn-like growth, itching, nail changes, numbness, open sore that won't heal\n  Favours second: abdominal distention, abdominal situs inversus, abnormal atrial arrangement, abnormal axonemal organization of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal central microtubular pair morphology of respiratory motile cilia\n\nRelated to Squamous Cell Carcinoma with Humoral Hypercalcemia (PTHrP-mediated): adenosquamous carcinoma, anal canal squamous cell carcinoma, anal carcinoma\n\nRelated to Breast Cancer with Bone Metastases: adult extraskeletal osteosarcoma, bone carcinoma, bone chondrosarcoma\n\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.61):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS: SPECIAL TESTS Differential diagnosis of hypercalcemia is best achieved by using clini cal criteria, but immunometric assays to measure PTH are especially useful in distinguishi\n[RAG context for 'A 45-year-old African American woman presents to her primary care physician for not feeling well' (score=0.62):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  Disorders of the Parathyroid Gland and Calcium Homeostasis The diagnosis is typically made by detecting an elevated immunoreactive PTH level in a patient with asymptomatic hypercalcemia (see “Differen\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.76):]\n  Source: Obstentrics_Williams\n  Hypercalcemia is caused by hyperparathyroidism or cancer in 90 percent of cases (Potts, 2015). Because many automated laboratory systems include serum calcium measurement, hyperparathyroidism has chan\n[RAG context for 'She states she has had a cough for the past week' (score=0.77):]\n  Source: Pathology_Robbins\n  Primary hyperparathyroidism is a common endocrine disorder and an important cause of hypercalcemia. There was a dramatic increase in the detection of cases in the latter half of the 20th century, main\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  poorly controlled GERD and antacid use → antacid-induced hypercalcemia → Reactive Hypercalcemia (e.g., Antacid Overuse or Exogenous Causes)\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Investigate serum calcium levels and antacid dosage history to confirm antacid-induced hypercalcemia as the mechanism\n  non-smoker status with new-onset cough and constitutional symptoms → atypical presentation of lung malignancy → Squamous Cell Carcinoma with Humoral Hypercalcemia (PTHrP-mediated)\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for PTHrP levels and perform imaging to rule out non-smoking-related lung malignancy despite low prior probability\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
